# Critical Findings 🚨

### [Critical] Middleware Fake JWT Forgery (Unverified Decoding)
**Location:** `middleware.ts:24-35` (`decodeJwtPayload`)
**Risk:** The frontend middleware determines the user's route (e.g., `/executive/dashboard`) by extracting the `token` cookie and decoding it via a simple Base64 decode (`atob`). **It does not cryptographically verify the JWT signature.** Because the `token` cookie is set via client-side JavaScript (`document.cookie`) and is not `HttpOnly`, an attacker can simply craft a fake JWT with `{"role": "CEO"}`, update their cookie in the browser console, and instantly bypass the middleware route protections to access CEO-only pages.

### [High] LocalStorage Role Tampering (Cosmetic Component Gates)
**Location:** `src/store/auth.ts` and `src/hooks/use-permissions.ts`
**Risk:** The entire UI component gating system (showing/hiding buttons, admin panels, approval queues) relies on `useAuthStore`, which is persisted in `localStorage`. Any user can open Chrome DevTools, edit `localStorage.getItem("auth-storage")`, and change their role to `"CEO"`. This instantly tricks the `usePermissions` hook into granting them client-side access to all executive UI components, forms, and approval screens. Combined with the backend IDOR vulnerabilities, this gives them full graphical access to exploit the system.
